%I A399511 #30 Sep 21 2026 01:06:39
%S A399511 1,89,517,9163,256025,20825,581875,2541875,3399375,2113125,380625,
%T A399511 931875,1674750,4100250,7368900,18041100,32423160,79380840,142661904,
%U A399511 349275696,359114448,113145648,897786120,282864120,2244465300,707160300,5611163250,1767900750,14027908125,4419751875
%N A399511 Trajectory of 1 under Conway's PIGAME.
%C A399511 See A395539 for the definition of the PIGAME successor function and many related references.
%C A399511 Since a(1) = 89*2^k with k = 0, it is expected that the next power of two in the sequence is 2^pi(0), where pi(0) = 3 is the 0th decimal (i.e., integer part) of Pi. Indeed, after 775 iterations we get a(776) = 8 = 2^3.
%H A399511 M. F. Hasler, <a href="/A399511/b399511.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..1000</a>
%F A399511 a(n+1) = A395539(a(n)) for all n >= 0.
%e A399511 a(0) = 1 is the initial value, then a(1) = A395539(1) = 89 is the result from applying Conway's PIGAME function to this initial value.
%e A399511 The next value is a(2) = A395539(a(1)) = A395539(89) = 517. This value can be found in the b-file, but we can also search for the first denominator (A350556) that divides a(1), which is A350556(15) = 89. We divide by this and multiply with the corresponding numerator A350555(15) = 517 = 11 * 47, to get this value for a(2).
%e A399511 Next, to get a(3) = A395539(a(2) = 11 * 47), we find that A350556(25) = 47 is the first denominator by which we can divide, then we multiply with A350555(25) = 833 = 7^2 * 17, to get a(3) = 7^2 * 11 * 17 = 9163. Subsequent values are:
%e A399511 a(4) = 5^2 * 7^2 * 11 * 19 = 256025,
%e A399511 a(5) = 5^2 * 7^2 * 17 = 20825,
%e A399511 a(6) = 5^4 * 7^2 * 19 = 581875,
%e A399511 a(7) = 5^4 * 7^2 * 83 = 2541875,
%e A399511 a(8) = 3 * 5^4 * 7^2 * 37 = 3399375,
%e A399511 a(9) = 3 * 5^4 * 7^2 * 23 = 2113125,
%e A399511 a(10) = 3 * 5^4 * 7 * 29 = 380625.
%e A399511 Later values are:
%e A399511 a(100) = 3^8 * 5^3 * 7^2 * 11^3 * 23 = 1230217844625,
%e A399511 a(200) = 3^2 * 5^2 * 7^12 * 11^9 * 447943253166003144678975.
%e A399511 a(500) = 5 * 7^9 * 11^32 * 37 = 15762315407752375895952399630194565793983695.
%e A399511 a(776) = 8 = 2^3 is the first power of 2 to occur after the initial value. This is the final result of PIGAME, in the sense of Conway's Theorem 2, cf. comments.
%e A399511 But we can continue to iterate the PIGAME successor function, which is well defined for all integers, given the last denominator equal to 1.
%e A399511 Actually, after a(776) = 2^3, we get a(777) = 2^3 * 89, which starts the sequence that will compute pi(3) = 1, the third decimal digit of Pi. We then find:
%e A399511 a(1000) = 2^2 * 3^6 * 5^52 * 7^6 * 11^8 * 89,
%e A399511 a(10^4) = 2^2 * 3 * 5^1479 * 7^16 * 11^565 * 89,
%e A399511 a(10^5) = 2 * 3^98 * 5^1012 * 7^4 * 11^15876 * 19,
%e A399511 a(10^6) = 2 * 3^95 * 5^212914 * 7^10 * 11^24613 * 19, and so on.
%t A399511 pigame = {365/46, 29/161, 79/575, 679/451, 3159/413, 83/407, 473/371, 638/355, 434/335, 89/235, 17/209, 79/122, 31/183, 41/115, 517/89, 111/83, 305/79, 23/73, 73/71, 61/67, 37/61, 19/59, 89/57, 41/53, 833/47, 53/43, 86/41, 13/38, 23/37, 67/31, 71/29, 83/19, 475/17, 59/13, 41/291, 1/7, 1/11, 1/1024, 1/97, 89};
%t A399511 NestList[SelectFirst[#*pigame, IntegerQ] &, 1, 50] (* _Paolo Xausa_, Sep 20 2026 *)
%o A399511 (PARI) A399511_upto(N, a=1)=vector(N+1, i, a=if(i>1, A395539(a), a)) \\ Use optional arg 'a' to get the trajectory of a different initial value. To go beyond n >> 10^5 it is more efficient to use the list of exponents instead of the product p(k)^e(k).
%o A399511 (Python)
%o A399511 A399511=lambda upto=99:[(t:=A395539(t)if i else 1)for i in range(upto)]
%Y A399511 Cf. A395539 (definition of the PIGAME successor function), A399512 - A399514 (similar: trajectories of 2, 3 and 4).
%K A399511 nonn,easy,new
%O A399511 0,2
%A A399511 _M. F. Hasler_, Sep 11 2026
